ry Sage-King","identityHints":{"nativePlaces":[],"relatedLocalPeople":["chenshu:002:p002","chenshu:002:p018"],"activeDateHints":["legendary antiquity"],"polityHints":["Yu"]},"claims":[["chenshu:002:c0012","name",{"kind":"personal-name","en":"Chonghua","zh":"重華","pinyin":"Chonghua"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0006"]],["chenshu:002:c0013","name",{"kind":"alternate-name","en":"Shun","zh":"舜","pinyin":"Shun"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0006","chenshu:002:s0010"]],["chenshu:002:c0014","name",{"kind":"epithet","en":"Yu","zh":"虞"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0010","chenshu:002:s0044"]],["chenshu:002:c0015","role",{"roleId":"ruler"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0006","chenshu:002:s0010","chenshu:002:s0044"]],["chenshu:002:c0542","sex",{"sex":"male"},"strongly-inferred",["chenshu:002:s0006"]],["chenshu:002:c0543","historicity",{"historicity":"legendary"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0006"]],["chenshu:002:c0544","attestation",{"qualitative":"legendary antiquity"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0006","chenshu:002:s0010"]],["chenshu:002:c0545","family-relationship",{"relation":"parent-of","personId":"chenshu:002:p018","kinshipTerm":{"zh":"父","en":"father"},"parentage":"biological","subjectRole":"father","objectRole":"son"},"strongly-inferred",["chenshu:002:s0044"]],["chenshu:002:c0546","event-participation",{"kind":"political","role":"exemplar","action":"yielded-toward-Xia"},"explicit",["chenshu:002:s0010"]]],"mentions":[["chenshu:002:m0011","s0006","personal-name",{"zh":[],"en":[{"exact":"Shun","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":8,"endCodePoint":12,"unitTextHash":"sha256:79f25f503527a1f5eff37485841ab0b205aaa3ee08f85f0bcd2efcd6d3a2063f"}]},null],["chenshu:002:m0013","s0006","personal-name",{"zh":[{"exact":"重華","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":3,"endCodePoint":5,"unitTextHash":"sha256:87ee0bedc1c6d69f3e9ddb60c909b69c6f149d0980a7b9b7b6e52fa5d85c10dc"}],"en":[]},null],["chenshu:002:m0020","s0010","personal-name",{"zh":[],"en":[{"exact":"Shun","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":224,"endCodePoint":228,"unitTextHash":"sha256:94be8b135b50618b4ccb880d3d852cd0bd850c8819d733293eee8ac1cdb788fb"}]},null],["chenshu:002:m0022","s0010","alternate-name",{"zh":[{"exact":"虞","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":33,"endCodePoint":34,"unitTextHash":"sha256:64e7f06a50b172c3646f25e931cea1f75a83236509f7b443cd9e0ee687dbe1c7"}],"en":[]},null],["chenshu:002:m0056","s0044","alternate-name",{"zh":[],"en":[{"exact":"Yu","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":71,"endCodePoint":73,"unitTextHash":"sha256:47f4f63d4eafb8b4cab6a737645d3cdae15a5c38c9e77d93b5f0104f90aea2a5"}]},null],["chenshu:002:m0060","s0044","alternate-name",{"zh":[{"exact":"虞","occurrence":0,"startCodePoint":17,"endCodePoint":18,"unitTextHash":"sha256:d5ca2adc27856d71269a2453d11037ebb7e8b13b1f3f05bc2968babbce671c7e"}],"en":[]},null]]}],"units":[{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0001","zh":"志九十五 食貨一 明末，苛政紛起，籌捐增餉，民窮財困。","en":"Treatise 95: Food and Money, Part 1. In the closing years of the Ming, oppressive policies multiplied—special levies were raised and military allotments increased—until both the populace and the treasury were drained.","literal":"Food and Money 1. At the end of the Ming, harsh policies arose one after another; fundraising levies and increased military provisions were imposed, and the people were impoverished while state finances were exhausted."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0002","zh":"有清入主中國，概予蠲除，與民更始。","en":"Once the Qing assumed rule over China, they swept these burdens away wholesale and let the people begin anew.","literal":"When the Qing entered and ruled China, they abolished these measures entirely and gave the people a fresh beginning."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0003","zh":"逮康、乾之世，國富民殷。","en":"By the Kangxi and Qianlong reigns, the empire was flush and the people at ease.","literal":"By the eras of Kangxi and Qianlong, the state was rich and the people prosperous."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0004","zh":"凡滋生人丁，永不加賦，又普免天下租稅，至再至三。","en":"No matter how much the population grew, the poll tax was never raised; land taxes across the realm were remitted again and again—two and three times over.","literal":"For all newly born population, taxation was never increased; moreover, land taxes throughout the empire were universally remitted, again and again."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0005","zh":"嗚呼，古未有也。","en":"Truly, nothing like it had been seen in all of history.","literal":"Alas—such a thing had never existed in antiquity."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0006","zh":"道、咸以降，海禁大開，國家多故。","en":"After the Daoguang and Xianfeng periods, the maritime ban was largely lifted, and the dynasty was beset by one crisis after another.","literal":"From the Daoguang and Xianfeng reigns onward, maritime prohibitions were greatly relaxed, and the state faced many troubles."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0007","zh":"耗財之途廣，而生財之道滯。","en":"Avenues for spending money multiplied, while ways of producing it stalled.","literal":"Paths for spending wealth widened, while means for generating wealth stagnated."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0008","zh":"當軸者昧於中外大勢，召禍興戎，天府太倉之蓄，一旦蕩然，賠償兵費至四百餘兆。","en":"Men at the helm failed to grasp the larger forces shaping China and the world; they courted calamity and war until the imperial storehouses were emptied overnight and war reparations alone ran to more than four hundred million taels.","literal":"Those in power were ignorant of the great trends at home and abroad; they invited disaster and raised armies. The reserves of the Heavenly Treasury and the Grand Granary were swept away in a single stroke, and military indemnities reached more than four hundred million."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0009","zh":"以中國所有財產抵借外債，積數十年不能清償。","en":"The nation's assets were pledged to service foreign loans, and decades of payments still could not clear the debt.","literal":"Using all of China's wealth as collateral to borrow foreign debt, repayment could not be cleared even after decades."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0010","zh":"攤派加捐，上下交困。","en":"Assessments were spread and surcharges piled on until court and country alike were squeezed dry.","literal":"Apportionments and added levies were imposed; both the upper and lower levels were trapped in hardship."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0011","zh":"乃改海運以節漕費，變圜法以行國幣，講鹽政以增歲入，開礦產以擴財源。","en":"The government turned to sea transport to cut grain-shipping costs, overhauled currency to put a national coinage in circulation, reworked the salt monopoly to swell annual receipts, and opened mines to broaden the revenue base.","literal":"Thereupon they changed to sea transport to economize on grain-tribute shipping costs, altered the currency system to issue national coinage, reformed salt administration to increase annual revenue, and opened mineral resources to expand fiscal sources."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0012","zh":"以及創鐵路，改郵傳，設電局，通海舶。","en":"Railways were built, the postal system modernized, telegraph bureaus established, and steam shipping brought into regular service.","literal":"Moreover they established railways, reformed the postal relay system, set up telegraph offices, and opened communication by seagoing vessels."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0013","zh":"新政繁興，孳孳謀利，而於古先聖王生眾食寡、為疾用舒之道，昧焉不講。","en":"New policies multiplied and officials strained every nerve for gain, while the sage-kings' counsel—to increase the multitude and lessen consumption, to produce with urgency and spend with ease—was left unheeded.","literal":"New policies proliferated, and they strove unceasingly to seek profit; yet regarding the way of the ancient sage-kings—to increase the people and reduce consumption, and to produce with urgency while spending with ease—they were ignorant and did not discuss it."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0014","zh":"夫以唐、虞治平之世，而其告舜、禹也，諄諄以「四海困窮，天祿永終」為戒。","en":"Even in the golden age of Yao and Shun, their charge to Shun and Yu rang with one warning: \"When the four seas are impoverished, Heaven's mandate is cut off forever.\"","literal":"Consider—even in the well-governed age of Tang and Yu, in instructing Shun and Yu they earnestly took as a warning: \"When the four seas are destitute, Heaven's blessings will end forever.\""},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0015","zh":"有國者其可忽哉！","en":"How can any ruler of a realm afford to ignore this!","literal":"Can those who hold a state possibly neglect this!"},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0016","zh":"茲取清代理財始末，條著於篇。","en":"What follows sets out the full arc of Qing revenue policy, arranged section by section.","literal":"Here we take the beginning and end of Qing fiscal administration and set them forth in sections within this treatise."},{"book":"qingshigao","chapter":"120","id":"s0017","zh":"戶口 田制","en":"Household Registers and Land Tenure","literal":"Household Registers; Land System"},{"book":"beiqishu","chapter":"025","id":"s0205","zh":"伏願陛下哀忠念舊，愛孤恤寡，矜愚嘉善，舍過記功，敦骨肉之情，廣寬仁之路，思堯、舜之風，慕禹、湯之德，克己復禮，以成美化，天下幸甚。」","en":"I venture to hope Your Majesty will pity the loyal and remember the old, cherish the orphaned and comfort the widowed, pity the foolish and praise the good, pardon faults and record merit, thicken kinship among flesh and bone, broaden the path of generous forbearance, think of the manner of Yao and Shun, admire the virtue of Yu and Tang, restrain yourself and return to ritual to complete a transformation of culture — all under Heaven would be greatly blessed.\"","literal":"I venture to hope Your Majesty will pity the loyal and remember the old, cherish the orphaned and comfort the widowed, pity the foolish and praise the good, pardon faults and record merit, thicken kinship among flesh and bone, broaden the path of generous forbearance, think of the manner of Yao and Shun, admire the virtue of Yu and Tang, restrain yourself and return to ritual, to complete a transformation of culture — all under Heaven would be greatly blessed.\""},{"book":"chenshu","chapter":"002","id":"s0006","zh":"放勛、重華之世，咸無意於受終，當塗、典午之君，雖有心於揖讓，皆以英才處萬乘，高勳御四海，故能大庇黔首，光宅區縣。","en":"Yao and Shun took the throne without seeking it; the rulers of Wei and Jin, though they spoke of abdication, still set men of rare talent upon the throne and men of towering merit at the helm of the realm—thus they sheltered the people and brought order to the land.","literal":"In the ages of Fangxun and Chonghua, both were without intent to receive the end; the lords of Dangtu and Dianwu, though they had mind for yielding, all alike placed men of outstanding talent upon the ten-thousand chariots, with high merit steering the four seas—therefore they could greatly shelter the black-haired people and brightly settle the districts and counties."},{"book":"chenshu","chapter":"002","id":"s0010","zh":"既而煙雲表色，日月呈瑞，緯聚東井，龍見譙邦，除舊布新，既彰玄象，遷虞事夏，且協謳訟，[2]九域八荒，同布衷款，百神群祀，皆有誠願。","en":"Then clouds and mist took auspicious hues; sun and moon showed signs; stars clustered at the Eastern Well; a dragon was seen in Qiao. The old order gave way to the new, and Heaven's signs were plain; as in the transfer from Shun to Yu, the people's acclaim agreed. [2] Within the nine provinces and beyond the eight directions, loyal petitions poured in; every spirit and rite was offered in earnest devotion.","literal":"Thereafter clouds and mist showed color; sun and moon presented omens; constellations gathered at the Eastern Well; a dragon appeared in the Qiao region. Removing the old and spreading the new, the dark signs were already manifest; shifting from Yu and serving Xia, and moreover matching the songs of praise, [2] the nine domains and eight wilds alike spread forth sincere petitions; the hundred spirits and assembled sacrifices all had sincere wish."},{"book":"chenshu","chapter":"002","id":"s0044","zh":"梁氏以天祿斯改，期運永終，欽若唐、虞，推其鼎玉，朕東西退讓，拜手陳辭，避舜子於箕山之陽，求支伯於滄洲之野，而公卿敦逼，率土翹惶，天命難稽，遂享嘉祚。","en":"The House of Liang, its mandate spent, its turn complete—like Tang and Yu they offered up the regalia. We yielded east and west, bowed with joined hands and pleaded our case, hiding like Shun's son on Mount Ji's sunny slope, seeking Zhibo on Cangzhou's wilds—but ministers pressed us and the realm looked up in dread. Heaven's mandate would not wait; thus We received the glorious throne.","literal":"The House of Liang, its heavenly blessing now changed, its allotted span ended forever—reverently in accord with Tang and Yu, they pushed forth their tripod and jade. We yielded east and west, bowed with joined hands and spoke our plea, avoiding Shun's son on the sunny slope of Mount Ji, seeking Zhibo on the wilds of Cangzhou—but the dukes and ministers pressed urgently; the whole land looked up in alarm. Heaven's mandate could not be delayed; thus We received the fine fortune."}],"candidateDossiers":[{"id":"per_09MBAZF9HC1EMPWKHPJY","version":"sha256:8bb250cb4e24ef7a90c45022b4708ecb49f3a61ee60e5f4c8f010add4432625c","acceptedSourceMembers":1,"names":[["zh:匋唐",["陶唐"],["personal","preferred"]]]},{"id":"per_0S5YX23Q3KWP38Z63E1S","version":"sha256:9902d480d8a704e185361102bd6bd38139f5ec3515847c81f690af12d3c7eeea","acceptedSourceMembers":1,"names":[["zh:匋唐",["陶唐"],["clan","preferred"]]]},{"id":"per_35F1MHFCBWN052YCDXVD","version":"sha256:32bfe70f60b6d18a8526d38e18b81d45f71082ede754c28d9389e4e42ede9bca","acceptedSourceMembers":1,"names":[["zh:匋唐",["陶唐"],["alternate"]],["zh:堯",["堯"],["preferred"]]]},{"id":"per_KFSR9J2S28VP913593C9","version":"sha256:51649911a754f5c5e6d8a115a4df3391b1ce5bcf89fb9f404ce1110c439c6e3e","acceptedSourceMembers":1,"names":[["zh:匋唐",["陶唐"],["epithet","preferred","regnal"]]]},{"id":"per_QRR8YHN1D3492ZMJEZTS","version":"sha256:442e88cd280a187068370b1d247e5330d80c4efa3966622fa05c9562195d5a3f","acceptedSourceMembers":1,"names":[["zh:匋唐",["陶唐"],["alternate"]],["zh:堯",["堯"],["personal","preferred"]]]},{"id":"per_Y2PF2T3CQC1T40YQ4ZWX","version":"sha256:8478a2ad5237229bb9ad3419b8c78b69a455